
                              POST-SOLUTION ANALYSIS
                              ----------------------

Active Constraint - Independent Variable Pairings
=================================================
In this report dO/dZ is the derivative of the objective function with
respect to an independent variable and dW/dZ is the derivative of a dependent
variable with respect to an independent variable.

The following is a possible pairing of active dependent variable bounds with
independent variables.  It is based both on the derivatives of the dependent 
variables and of the objective function with respect to the independent variables.
                                                                                                                                                                  Kuhn-Tucker   Derivative     dO/dZ
                                                                                                                                                                  -----------   ----------   ----------
  1. Upper /S9:Flow["StdVapVol"]                                                                                                                                   -4.777E-04                          
       and /AirFlowControl/MO1:Setpoint                                                                                                                                          1.000E+00   -5.889E-06
  2. Lower /CURefrigerant:OffsetFromMaxRate                                                                                                                        -6.150E-01                          
       and /RefrigSystem/CT1/MO1:Setpoint                                                                                                                                        1.001E-01   -6.556E-02
  3. Upper /S6:Liq:SumMoleFrac                                                                                                                                      6.356E-01                          
       and /S6:Equil:PFrac["Liq"]                                                                                                                                               -1.737E+01   -8.807E+00
Solution Sensitivity to Active Constraints
==========================================
This section lists the sensitivity of the solution to the 
active constraints.  For each constraint the amount that 
each independent variable would be expected to change for 
a one unit relaxation of the constraint is listed. 
This is an estimate based on a QP approximation to the 
nonlinear optimization problem.  In addition, the amount 
each constraint could be relaxed before the active set of 
constraints would change is estimated.  The active set would
change if either it would no longer be optimal to make the 
constraint active or another constraint would become active.

The sensitivity of the solution (independent variables)
to the relaxation of the active constraint: 
   Lower /CURefrigerant:OffsetFromMaxRate
is as follows:
   /S6:Equil:PFrac["Liq"]                                                                                                                                    -3.235E-03
   /RefrigSystem/CT1/MO1:Setpoint                                                                                                                            -8.946E+00
It could be relaxed by   6.192E-04 before the active constraint 
   Lower /CURefrigerant:OffsetFromMaxRate
would no longer be active because it would not be optimal.
It could be relaxed by   1.306E-01 before the inactive constraint 
   Lower /S6:Equil:PFrac["Liq"]
would become active.

The sensitivity of the solution (independent variables)
to the relaxation of the active constraint: 
   Upper /S6:Liq:SumMoleFrac
is as follows:
   /S6:Equil:PFrac["Liq"]                                                                                                                                    -5.155E-02
   /RefrigSystem/CT1/MO1:Setpoint                                                                                                                             1.662E+01
It could be relaxed by  -3.129E-05 before the active constraint 
   Upper /S6:Liq:SumMoleFrac
would no longer be active because it would not be optimal.
It could be relaxed by   8.198E-03 before the inactive constraint 
   Lower /S6:Equil:PFrac["Liq"]
would become active.

The sensitivity of the solution (independent variables)
to the relaxation of the active constraint: 
   Upper /S9:Flow["StdVapVol"]
is as follows:
   /S6:Equil:PFrac["Liq"]                                                                                                                                     1.183E-05
   /AirFlowControl/MO1:Setpoint                                                                                                                               1.000E+00
   /RefrigSystem/CT1/MO1:Setpoint                                                                                                                            -8.966E-03
It could be relaxed by   3.096E+01 before the active constraint 
   Lower /CURefrigerant:OffsetFromMaxRate
would no longer be active because it would not be optimal.
It could be relaxed by   2.103E-04 before the inactive constraint 
   Upper /AirFlowControl/MO1:Setpoint
would become active.

Objective Function Sensitivities to Independent Variables
=========================================================
This report lists the effect of moving each independent, while keeping all 
other independent variables constant, on the objective function.  Each 
effect is broken down into individual affects on variables which explicitly 
affect the objective function.  In this report dO/dX is the partial
derivative of the objective function with respect to a variable and dX/dZ
is the derivative of a variable with respect to an independent variable.
Note: The Total Derivative printed out for each variable should equal
      the Reduced Objective Function Gradient value listed on the final
      iteration! If this is NOT the case, rerun the problem again with
      tighter convergence tolerances.

